#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""hypr-gamepad: a gamepad drives Hyprland.

The aim: herdr's agents, voice-to-text dictation, and every window, tab, herdr pane and workspace,
driven from the controller alone, with the hands off the keyboard. Made for the DualSense.

    hypr-gamepad run [--dry] [--device PATH]   the daemon; hypr-gamepad.service runs it
    hypr-gamepad status                        the pad, its battery, Hyprland, the config file
    hypr-gamepad check                         the config, then every action builds in the running
                                               Hyprland (runs none)
    hypr-gamepad map [--json]                  what each control does, from LAYOUT and the config

Each takes --config PATH. Without it the config is $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hypr-gamepad/config.toml
(~/.config/hypr-gamepad/config.toml), and no file there means the defaults described here:
config.example.toml lists every key with its default, setups/ holds whole setups people run. A key
the program does not know is a warning; a wrong type, or a control or action it does not know, stops
it with the key named.

Inside the focused window (herdr, here or on another machine; a browser; a board app; any window):

    Cross        Enter: send, confirm
    Circle       Esc: back, stop the agent
    Square       take back: delete the word before the cursor (Ctrl+W in herdr and terminals,
                 Ctrl+Backspace elsewhere); held, it keeps deleting, faster and faster. Never Esc:
                 a running agent keeps running
    D-pad        arrow keys (held: they repeat)
    L1 / R1      previous / next tab: herdr's, the browser's, a board's, a window group's
    Left stick   in herdr, the keys [herdr.keys] gives agent_prev / agent_next (up / down) and
                 space_prev / space_next (left / right): by default herdr's own pane keys, so the
                 pane above, below, to the left, to the right. The browser: back / forward (left /
                 right); a board: the tile before / after
    L3           Shift+Tab: the agent's mode (plan, accept edits)
    R3           normal text size in the focused window (Ctrl+0), in either layer
    Right stick  scroll, smoothly: the further it tilts, the faster

herdr's keys ([herdr]) are written in herdr's own syntax and default to herdr 0.9.5's: prefix Ctrl+B,
tabs prefix+p / prefix+n, panes prefix+k / j / h / l, zoom prefix+z, the latest notification
prefix+o. A key set to "" does nothing in herdr. A window is herdr's by its class, or a terminal with
herdr running under it, or a terminal titled as herdr titles its window; [windows] adds classes,
class prefixes, program names, terminals, browsers and the titles of a board app.

What the pad reaches beyond the window, each from its table in the config (off unless it says):

    Triangle     [jump]: herdr's jump key to a herdr window (default: prefix+o, the agent that called
                 last; elsewhere a note says it works inside herdr), or a command that lists the
                 agents that wait (again within 10 s: the next one)
    PS tap       [board]: a command, a board app's launcher
    PS held      [wheel]: a sessions wheel shell plugin on this screen: the right stick lights a
                 slot, let go of PS to show it; Circle closes it
    Create       [ideas]: a command, a voice ideas listener's toggle; unset, a note says so once

The map, in either layer:

    Options held what each control does in the focused window, on screen; a tap keeps it up until
                 the next one (the shell plugin ormus.hypr-gamepad-map)

Talk, in either layer ([talk]: voice-flow by default, or a command each to start, stop and cancel,
or none):

    R2 held      talk; let go and the words land in the focused window
    R2 tap       keeps listening until the next tap
    Mic button   talk: a press starts, the next press pastes (needs the udev rule below)
    Circle       while talking: drop the recording, nothing is pasted

The pad's lights (needs the udev rule below):

    lightbar     gold at the desk, flare red while talking, cream while L2 holds the desktop side,
                 blue in game mode
    player light which screen has focus, as a PS5 numbers its players: one, two, three, four

The udev rule (udev/60-hypr-gamepad.rules, installed by hand into /etc/udev/rules.d) lets the user
at the seat set the lights and read the raw reports, where the mic button is. Without it the pad
still works, without lights and mic button; `hypr-gamepad status` says.

Games:

    PS + Options game mode on / off: the daemon lets go of the pad, so a game reads it; nothing else
                 acts until the same chord brings it back (a long rumble going, a double coming back)

L2 held is the world layer, the desktop:

    D-pad        focus the window in that direction, across screens
    L1 / R1      previous / next workspace on this screen
    Left stick   focus the screen in that direction; with none beside, left / right go to the
                 screen before / after (screens stacked in a column have none beside)
    Right stick  up / down: bigger / smaller text in the focused window (Ctrl+= / Ctrl+-: foot and
                 the herdr windows, browsers); held, it keeps going. A tilt held from scrolling
                 when L2 goes down waits for the stick to come back first
    Cross        full screen on / off
    Circle       back to the workspace before
    Square       next window on this workspace
    Triangle     scratchpad on / off

The layout ([layout.base], [layout.world]) sets any one control to any action by name (`map --json`
names the controls, ACTIONS the actions; zoom, herdr's pane zoom, is bound to none by default); ""
unbinds it. Only the controls listed change. A control is named by where it sits on the pad, as the
kernel's gamepad spec does: the face buttons south, east, west, north (Cross, Circle, Square,
Triangle), the middle ones select, start, home (Create, Options, PS); the DualSense's names work too.

The daemon grabs the pad, so no other program reads it while it runs; game mode (PS + Options)
gives it to a game. Each press becomes one action from the layout, sent to Hyprland over its request
socket as a Lua dispatcher: 0.5 ms a call, where hyprctl takes 16 ms (measured on a test machine,
2026-10-29). Nothing is typed into the desktop through uinput: a key for a window goes to that window
through Hyprland's send_shortcut, which hands the window the key and gives focus back in the same
call. --dry prints each action instead of sending it, and reads Hyprland only to look.
"""
